  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "Grad-CAM visualisations showed that even with improved pipeline preprocessing, the classification model frequently relied on visual features located outside the boundaries of the target building.",
      "what": "The authors built an automated street view imagery processing pipeline that filters images for task suitability and aligns targets using camera metadata and parcel boundaries without requiring building footprints. Tested on 10,311 images across 1,609 detached-house parcels in South Korea for vacant house assessment, target-house alignment agreed with human labels on 85.6% of images compared to 79.8% for standard rules. Combining adaptive filtering and target alignment increased vacancy classification AUROC from 0.684 to 0.719.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that classification performance metrics alone can obscure target identification errors, establishing a practical method to make image selection and building targets explicitly verifiable for automated and human virtual audits."
    }
  },
